WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.960
Hi, good morning Hank, it's, uh,

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.759
Friday? I think? Uh, today I'm not going to do a real video.

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:21.329
I'm going to do a live show video, but Nerdfighters, before you start to punish me, I have permission from Hank to do this.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.340
It's going to save me a lot of time not to have to edit a video today, and I appreciate your patience,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.969
But I am going to read to you the first chapter of "The Fault In Our Stars" today,

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.180
Which is something I wanted to...I've wanted to do for a long time, but, um,

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.029
and I tried to do once, like a few months ago,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:40.410
But then I had to change a bunch...I wanted to change a bunch of stuff, so I ended up deleting that live show

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.610
But this live show is here to stay. I'm sorry that it's going to be longer than 4 minutes

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.610
But you know it's a long book um

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:51.839
And I'm gonna start reading to you now unless there's anything that I forgot to say um

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:58.139
Yeah, oh, I guess just the only other thing is that for those of you

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.879
Who are watching live can you please explain to people who are coming into the room for the first time?

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.159
that they haven't missed the entire thing because it's going to be recorded and they can go back and listen to the first chapter at

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:12.419
their leisure, you know they can listen to it a hundred times if they want, it's a little weird, but you can do that.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:18.779
So this book is called "The Fault in our Stars", and it is dedicated to my friend Esther Earl.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.379
And then after the dedication comes the uh...

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:28.889
You know how like pretentious novels always start with you know pretentious quotations from pretentious novels?

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:36.509
This book is no exception. It starts with an epigraph: "As the tide washed in, the dutch tulip man faced the ocean.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.329
Conjoiner, rejoinder, poisoner, concealer, revelator. 'Look at it,'

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:46.829
He said, 'rising up and rising down, taking everything with it.'

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:53.760
'What's that?' Anna asked. 'Water,' the Dutchman said. 'Well, and time.'"

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.780
- Peter Van Houten,

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.780
An Imperial Affliction

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.879
Then there's an author's note. Which I will read to you now.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:11.759
Author's note: This is not so much an author's note as an author's reminder of what was printed in small type a few pages ago.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.539
This book is a work of fiction. I made it up.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.800
Neither novels nor their readers benefit from attempts to divine whether any facts hide inside of a story.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:30.700
Such efforts attack the very idea that made-up stories can matter, which is sort of the foundational assumption of our species.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.360
I appreciate your cooperation in this matter.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.529
And now begins the actual book:

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.000
The Fault in Our Stars

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.250
I'm reading from a page proof by the way, so there may be some mistakes.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.610
But hopefully not if there are I'm going to have to stop reading and correct myself.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.040
Chapter One:

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.400
Late in the winter of my sixteenth year, my mother decided

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.000
I was depressed,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.500
presumably because I rarely left the house, spent quite a lot of time in bed, read the same book over and over, ate

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.690
infrequently, and devoted quite a bit of my abundant free time to thinking about death.

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:16.859
Whenever you read a cancer booklet or website or whatever, they always list depression among the side effects of cancer

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.139
But in fact, depression is not a side effect of cancer. Depression is a side effect of dying.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.250
(Cancer is also a side effect of dying. Almost everything is, really.)

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:33.479
But my mom believed I required treatment, so she took me to see my Regular Doctor Jim, who agreed that I was veritably

00:03:33.639 --> 00:03:40.049
swimming in a paralyzing and totally clinical depression, and that therefore my meds should be adjusted and also

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:42.040
I should attend a weekly support group.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.200
This support group featured a rotating cast of characters in various states of tumor-driven unwellness.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.720
Why did the cast rotate? A side effect of dying.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.899
The support group, of course, was depressing as hell.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.819
It meant every Wednesday in the basement of a stone-walled Episcopal church shaped like a cross.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.949
We all sat right in the middle of the cross, where the two boards would have met, where the heart of Jesus would have been.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:13.649
I noticed this because Patrick, the support group leader and only person over 18 in the room, talked about the heart of Jesus

00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:22.139
every freaking meeting, all about how we, as young cancer survivors were sitting right in Christ's very sacred heart and

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:22.960
whatever.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:29.670
So here's how it went in God's heart: the six or seven or ten of us walked or wheeled in,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:33.240
grazed at a decrepit selection of cookies and lemonade,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:40.120
sat down in the Circle of Trust, and listened to Patrick recount for the thousandth time his

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.820
depressingly miserable life story - how he had cancer in his balls and they thought he was going to die

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.960
but then he didn't die and now here he is, a full-grown adult in a church basement in the

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.200
137th Nicest City in America, divorced, addicted to video games,

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.080
mostly friendless, eking out a meager living by exploiting his cancer-tastic past,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:10.500
slowly working his way toward a master's degree that will not improve his career prospects, waiting, as we all do, for the sword of Damocles

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.920
to give him the relief that he escaped lo those many years ago when cancer took both of his nuts but spared

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.720
what only the most generous soul would call his life. And you too might be so lucky!

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.280
Then we introduced ourselves: Name. age.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.730
Diagnosis. And how we're doing today. I'm Hazel, I'd say when they'd get to me.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.800
Sixteen. Thyroid originally but with an impressive

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.280
and long-settled colony in my lungs.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.040
And I'm doing okay. —By the way,

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.489
I should mention that the narrator of this novel is a girl named Hazel Grace Lancaster

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:49.170
And she's 16 and as you may have noticed I'm a 34 year old man. That's a little bit awkward,

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:55.439
I know, like having a 34 year old man read to you in the voice of a 16 year old girl, so just

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.260
get over it. Just let me read you the story.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.400
Sorry, I'm not going to say [extra gruffly] "and then I took off my bra" or anything, at least in this chapter.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.750
um...All right

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:14.250
Once we got around the circle, Patrick always asked if anyone wanted to share. And then began the circle jerk of support:

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:19.200
everyone talking about fighting and battling and winning and shrinking and scanning.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.770
To be fair to Patrick, he let us talk about dying too.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:27.029
But most of them weren't dying. Most would live into adulthood as Patrick had,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.479
(Which meant there was quite a lot of competitiveness about it, with everybody wanting to beat not only cancer itself,

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:37.319
but also the other people in the room. Like, I realize that this is irrational,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:43.589
but when they tell you that you have, say, a 20% chance of living five years the math kicks in and you figure

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:50.220
that's one in five... so you look around and think, as any healthy person would: I gotta outlast at last four of these bastards.)

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:58.799
The only redeeming facet of support group was this kid named Isaac a long faced skinny guy with straight blond hair swept over one eye.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.080
His eyes were the problem.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:08.280
He had some fantastically improbable eye cancer. One eye had been cut out when he was a kid, and now he wore the kind of thick

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:14.200
glasses that made both of his eyes (the real one and the glass one) preternaturally huge,

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:20.380
like his whole head was basically just this fake eye and this real eye staring at you

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.680
from what I could gather on the rare occasions when Isaac shared with the group a

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.689
recurrence had placed his remaining eye in some mortal peril.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.900
Isaac and I communicated almost exclusively through sighs. Each time someone discussed

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:41.309
Anti-cancer diets or snorting up ground Shark-fin or whatever, he'd glanced over at me and sigh ever so slightly.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.870
I'd shake my head microscopically and exhale in response.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.590
So support group blew, and after a few weeks,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:54.419
I grew to be rather kicking and screaming about the whole affair. In fact, on the Wednesday

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:56.310
I made the acquaintance of Augustus Waters,

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:00.989
I tried my level best to get out of support group while sitting on the couch with my mom in the

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:08.190
third leg of a 12-hour marathon of the previous season's America's Next Top Model, which admittedly I had already seen, but still.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.049
Me: "I refused to attend support group."

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.440
Mom: "You know one of the symptoms of depression is disinterest in activities."

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:21.320
Me: "Please just let me watch America's Next Top Model. It's an activity."

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:24.600
Mom: "Television is a passivity."

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.380
Me: "Ugh mom, please."

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:32.960
Mom: "Hazel you're a teenager. You're not a little kid anymore. You need to make friends, get out of the house, live your life."

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.719
Me: "If you want me to be a teenager, don't send me to support group.

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:41.099
Buy me a fake ID so I can go to clubs and drink vodka and take pot."

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.380
Mom: "You don't take pot for starters."

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.940
Me: "See, that's the kind of thing I would know if you got me a fake ID."

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.280
Mom: "You're going to support group."

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.120
Me: "Uggggggh."

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.200
Mom: "Hazel, you deserve a life."

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:05.000
That shut me up, although I failed to see how attendance at support group met the definition of life. Still, I agreed to go -- after

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:10.460
negotiating the right to record the one point five episodes of America's Next Top Model I'd be missing.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.430
I went to support group for the same reason that I'd once allowed nurses with a mere 18 months of graduate education

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.550
to poison me with exotically named chemicals:

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.100
I wanted to make my parents happy.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:26.510
There is only one thing in this world shittier than biting it from cancer when you're 16,

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.779
And that one thing is having a kid who bites it from cancer.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:35.539
Mom pulled into the circular driveway behind the church at 4:56.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.050
I pretended to fiddle with my oxygen tank for a second just to kill time.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.169
"You want me to carry it for you?" "No, it's fine." I said.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:45.800
The cylindrical green tank only weighed a few pounds,

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.560
and I had this little steel cart to wheel it around behind me.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.640
It delivered two liters of oxygen to me each minute through a canela,

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:59.300
a transparent tube that split just beneath my neck wrapped behind my ears and then reunited in my nostrils.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:06.349
The contraption was necessary because my lungs sucked at being lungs. "I love you," she said as I got out. "You too, mom,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:11.330
see you at six." "Make friends!" She said through the rolled down window as I walked away.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:17.210
I didn't want to take the elevator, because taking the elevator is a Last Days kind of activity at support group

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:23.719
so I took the stairs. I grabbed a cookie and poured some lemonade into a Dixie cup and then turned around.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.919
A boy was staring at me. I was quite sure I'd never seen him before.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:34.640
Long and leanly muscular, he dwarfed the molded plastic elementary school chair he was sitting in.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.200
Mahogany hair, straight and short. He looked my age,

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:40.180
maybe a year older,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:45.619
and he sat with his tailbone against the edge of the chair, his posture aggressively poor,

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:53.150
one hand half in a pocket of dark jeans. I looked away, suddenly conscious of my myriad insufficiencies.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.939
I was wearing old jeans which had once been tight

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:02.539
but now sagged in weird places and a yellow t-shirt advertising a band I didn't even like anymore. Also my hair:

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:06.760
I had this pageboy haircut, and I hadn't even bothered to like brush it.

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:10.420
Furthermore, I had ridiculously fat chipmunk cheeks, a side affect of treatment.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:16.960
I looked like a normally proportioned person with a balloon for a head that wasn't even to mention the cankle situation and yet--

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:24.170
I cut a glance to him and his eyes were still on me. It occurred to me why they call it eye contact.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:30.920
I walked into the circle and sat down next to Isaac, two seats away from the boy. I glanced again.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.540
He was still watching me. Look let me just say it, he was hot.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.460
A nonhot boy stares at you relentlessly

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:43.960
and it is at best, awkward and at worst, a form of assault. But a hot boy... well.

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.689
I pulled out my phone and clicked it. So it would display the time:

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:49.680
4:59

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:56.239
The circle filled in with the unlucky twelve to eighteens, and then Patrick started us out with the serenity prayer. "God

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.240
grant me the serenity to accept the things I cannot change

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:04.120
the courage to change the things I can, and the wisdom to know the difference."

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.120
The guy was still staring at me. I felt rather blushy.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:12.439
Finally I decided that the proper strategy was to stare back.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.749
Boys do not have a monopoly on the staring business, after all.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:20.269
So I looked him over as Patrick acknowledged for the thousandth time his ball-lessness etc., and soon

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.150
It was a staring contest. After a while the voice smiled

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:30.319
And then finally his blue eyes glanced away when he looked back at me, I flicked my eyebrows up to say "I win."

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:32.520
He shrugged.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.369
Patrick continued, and then finally it was time for the introductions. "Isaac perhaps, you'd like to go first today

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:39.370
I know you're facing a challenging week."

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.869
"Yeah", Isaac said, "I'm Isaac,

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:46.940
I'm 17 and it's looking like I have to get surgery in a couple weeks, after which I'll be blind,

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:51.319
Not to complain or anything because I know a lot of us have it worse, but yeah,

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:55.700
I mean, being blind does sort of suck. My girlfriend helps, though. And friends like Augustus."

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:00.499
He nodded toward the boy, who now had a name.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:06.380
"So yeah," Isaac continued. He was looking at his hands which he folded into each other like the top of a teepee.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.660
"There's nothing you can do about it."

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:12.720
"We're here for you Isaac," Patrick said. "Let Isaac hear it guys."

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.920
And then we all, in a monotone said, "we're here for you Isaac."

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:19.720
Michael was next.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:27.620
He was 12. He had leukemia. He'd always had leukemia. He was okay, or so he said. He'd taken the elevator.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:32.620
Lida was 16 and plenty pretty enough to be the object of the hot boy's eye.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:38.779
She was a regular in a long remission from appendiceal cancer. Which I hadn't previously known existed,

00:13:38.779 --> 00:13:43.879
She said - as she had every other time I'd attended support group - that she felt strong.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:51.859
Which felt like bragging to me as the oxygen drizzling nubs tickled my nostrils. There were five others before they got to him.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:59.080
He smiled a little when his turn came, his voice was low, smoky, and dead sexy.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.640
"My name is Augustus Waters," he said. "I'm 17.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:06.050
I had just a little touch of osteosarcoma about a year and a half ago

00:14:06.329 --> 00:14:12.409
But I'm just here today at Isaac's request." "And how are you feeling?" asked Patrick. " Oh, I'm grand."

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:18.050
Augustus waters smiled with a corner of his mouth. "I'm on a roller coaster that only goes up, friend."

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:22.639
When it was my turn, I said, "My name's Hazel. I'm 16.

00:14:23.339 --> 00:14:26.208
Thyroid with mets in my lungs. I'm okay."

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.870
The hour proceeded apace: Fights were recounted, battles won amid wars sure to be lost;

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:38.059
hope was clung to; families were both celebrated and denounced; it was agreed that friends just didn't get it;

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:45.619
Tears were shed; comfort proffered. Neither, Augustus Waters nor I spoke again until Patrick said,

00:14:46.199 --> 00:14:48.679
"Augustus, perhaps you'd like to share your fears with the group."

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:57.260
"My fears?" "Yes."  "I fear oblivion," he said, without a moment's pause."I fear it like the proverbial blind man

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:01.099
who's afraid of the dark." "Too soon," Isaac said, cracking a smile.

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:05.870
"Oh was that insensitive?" Augustus asked, "I'm sorry, I can be really blind to other people's feelings."

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:13.099
Isaac was laughing, but Patrick raised a chastening finger and said "Augustus, please. Let's return to you and your struggles.

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:17.239
You said you fear oblivion?"  "I did," Augustus answered.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:19.319
Patrick seemed lost.

00:15:19.319 --> 00:15:22.369
"Would, uh, would anyone like to speak to that?"

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:30.960
I hadn't been in proper school in three years, my parents were my two best friends. My third best friend was an author who did not know I existed.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:37.880
I was a fairly shy person, not the hand-raising type and yet, just this once, I decided to speak.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:42.620
I half raised my hand and Patrick, his delight evident, immediately said,

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:43.300
"Hazel!"

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:48.340
I was, I'm sure he assumed, opening up, becoming part of the group.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:54.600
I looked over at Augustus Waters, who looked back at me. You could almost see through his eyes, they were so blue.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:59.180
"There will come a time," I said, "When all of us are dead.

00:15:59.699 --> 00:16:03.378
All of us. There will come a time when there are no human beings

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:07.980
remaining to remember that anyone ever existed or that our species ever did anything.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:09.680
There will be no one left to remember

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.500
Aristotle or Cleopatra, let alone you.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:19.250
Everything that we did and built and wrote and thought and discovered will be forgotten and all of this" -- I gestured encompassingly --

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:24.769
"will have been for naught. Maybe that time is coming soon, and maybe it's millions of years away.

00:16:24.769 --> 00:16:27.289
But even if we survive the collapse of our sun,

00:16:27.289 --> 00:16:32.420
We will not survive forever. There was time before organisms experienced consciousness,

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:39.520
and there will be time after. And if the inevitability of human oblivion worries you, I encourage you to ignore it.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:43.080
God knows that's what everyone else does."

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.180
I'd learned this from my aforementioned third best friend,

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:49.400
Peter van Houten, the reclusive author of the novel 'An Imperial Affliction',

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.789
the book that was as close a thing as I had to a Bible.

00:16:53.189 --> 00:16:56.419
Peter van Houten was the only person I'd ever come across who seemed to

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.799
(A) Understand what it's like to be dying and (B) not have died.

00:17:01.439 --> 00:17:04.399
After I finished there was quite a long period of silence

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.880
as I watched a smile spread all the way across Augustus's face -

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:12.660
not the crooked little smile of the boy trying to be sexy while he stared at me,

00:17:12.689 --> 00:17:15.349
but his real smile, too big for his face.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:20.059
"Goddamn," Augustus said quietly,  "Aren't you something else?"

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.460
Neither of us said anything for the rest of support group

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:27.080
At the end we all had to hold hands and Patrick led us in a prayer.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:32.200
"Lord Jesus Christ, we are gathered here in Your heart, literally in Your heart,

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:37.309
as Cancer Survivors. You and You alone know us as we know ourselves.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:41.419
Guide us to life and the light through our times of trial.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:47.900
We pray for Isaac's eyes, for Michael's and Jamie's blood, for Augustus's bones,

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:50.720
for Hazel's lungs, for James's throat.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:57.199
We pray that You might heal us and that we might feel Your love, and Your peace, which passes all understanding.

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:01.909
And we remember in our hearts those whom we knew and loved who have gone home to You:

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:10.560
Maria and Kade and Joseph and Haley and Abigail and Angelina and Taylor and Gabriel..."

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.380
It was a long list. The world contains a lot of dead people, and

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:22.129
while Patrick droned on reading from the list from a sheet of paper because it was too long to memorise, I kept my eyes closed,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:23.750
trying to think prayerfully,

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:28.970
but mostly imagining the day when my name would find its way onto that list, all the way at the end

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.100
when everyone had stopped listening.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:36.560
When Patrick was finished, we said this stupid mantra together--living our best life today!--

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:42.120
and it was over. Augustus Waters pushed himself out of his chair and walked over to me.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:47.539
His gait was crooked like his smile. He towered over me, but he kept his distance

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:53.260
so I wouldn't have to crane my neck to look him in the eye. "What's your name?" He asked. "Hazel."

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:54.110
"No, your full name?"

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.520
"um, Hazel Grace Lancaster."

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:58.980
He was just about to say something else when Isaac walked up.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:02.840
"Hold on," Augustus said, raising a finger, and turned to Isaac.

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:08.090
"That was actually worse than you made it out to be."  "I told you it was bleak."  "Why do you bother with it?"

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:10.220
"I don't know. It kind of helps."

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:14.869
Augustus leaned in so he thought I couldn't hear. "She's a regular?"

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:18.889
I couldn't hear Isaac's comment, but Augustus responded, "I'll say!"

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:25.010
He clasped Isaac by both shoulders, and then took a half step away from him. "Tell hazel about clinic."

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.660
Isaac leaned a hand against the snack table and focused his huge eye on me.

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:31.180
"All right, so I went to clinic this morning

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:36.019
And I was telling my surgeon that I'd rather be deaf than blind and he said it doesn't work that way.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:39.080
And I was like 'Yeah, I realize it doesn't work that way,

00:19:39.080 --> 00:19:45.080
I'm just saying that I'd rather be deaf than blind if I had the choice, which I realize I don't have.'

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:50.440
and he said, 'Well, the good news is you won't be deaf.' And I was like "Yeah, hey,

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:53.560
Thanks for explaining that my eye cancer isn't going to make me deaf.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:59.929
I feel so fortunate that an intellectual giant like yourself would deign to operate on me.'"  "He sounds like a winner,"

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:03.889
I said, "I'm try to give me some eye cancer just so I can make that guy's acquaintance."

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:08.780
"Good luck with that," Isaac said, "All right, I should go. Monica's waiting for me upstairs.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:10.900
I gotta look at her a lot while I can."

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:14.040
"Counterinsurgence tomorrow?" Augustus asked.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:18.660
"Definitely." Isaac turned and ran up the stairs, taking them two at a time.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.760
Augustus Waters turned to me. "Literally," he said. "Literally?" I asked.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:31.780
"We are literally in the heart of Jesus,"  he said, "I thought we were in a church basement, but we are literally in the heart of Jesus."

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:37.400
"Someone should tell Jesus," I said, "I mean it's got to be dangerous storing children with cancer in your heart."

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:41.299
"I would tell him myself," Augustus said, "but unfortunately

00:20:41.299 --> 00:20:45.380
I am literally stuck inside of his heart, so he won't be able to hear me."

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:46.279
I laughed.

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:53.390
He shook his head. Just looking at me. "What?" I asked. "Nothing," he said. "Why are you looking at me like that?"

00:20:54.330 --> 00:21:00.199
Augustus half smiled. "Because you're beautiful. I enjoy looking at beautiful people, and I decided a while ago

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:03.760
not to deny myself the simpler pleasures of existence."

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:05.809
A brief awkward silence ensued.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:14.059
Augustus plowed through. "I mean particularly given that as you so deliciously pointed out all of this will end in oblivion and everything..."

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:20.220
I kind of scoffed or sighed or exhaled in a way that was meant to be vaguely cough-y, and then said, "I'm not a beaut--"

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:24.760
"You're like a millennial Natalie Portman, like a 'V for Vendetta' Natalie Portman."

00:21:25.530 --> 00:21:32.149
"Never seen it," I said. "Really?" he asked, "Pixie haired gorgeous girl dislikes authority and can't help but fall for a boy

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:35.719
she knows his trouble. It's your autobiography as far as I can tell."

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:39.829
His every syllable flirted. Honestly, he kind of turned me on.

00:21:39.829 --> 00:21:44.020
I didn't even know that guys could turn me on, not like in Real Life.

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:50.700
A younger girl walked past us. "How's it going, Elisa?" he asked. She smiled and mumbled "Hi, Augustus."

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:56.140
"Memorial people," he explained. Memorial was the big research hospital. "Where do you go?"

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:00.820
"Children's," I said my voice smaller than I expected it to be.

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:05.060
He nodded. The conversation seemed over. "Well..." I said,

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:08.140
nodding vaguely toward the steps that led us out of the literal heart of Jesus.

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:11.380
I tilted my card onto its wheels and started walking.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:19.580
He limped beside me. "So see you next time, maybe?" I asked. "You should see it," He said, "V for Vendetta, I mean."

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:24.420
"Okay," I said, "I'll look it up." "No, with me, at my house, now," he said.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:29.560
I stopped walking. "I hardly know you, Augustus Waters. You could be an ax murderer."

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:32.900
He nodded. "True enough, Hazel Grace."

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:36.980
He walked past me, his shoulders filling out his green knit polo shirt

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:39.869
His back straight, his steps lilting

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:45.810
Just slightly to the right as he walked steady and confident on what I had determined was a prosthetic leg.

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:51.640
Osteosarcoma sometimes takes a limb or two to check you out, and if it likes you, it takes the rest.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:59.460
I followed him upstairs losing ground as I made my way up slowly, stairs not being a field of expertise for my lungs,

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:01.680
and then we were out of Jesus's heart,

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:08.819
and in the parking lot. The spring air, just on the cold side of perfect, the late afternoon light heavenly in its hurtfulness.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:14.069
Mom wasn't there yet, which was unusual because mom was almost always waiting for me.

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:20.790
I glanced around and saw that a tall curvy brunette girl had Isaac pinned against the stone wall of the church,

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:26.670
kissing him rather aggressively. They were close enough to me that I could hear the weird noises of their mouths together

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:31.560
and I could hear him saying "Always," and her saying "Always," in return.

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:36.569
Suddenly standing next to me, Augustus half whispered, "They're big believers in PDA."

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:41.939
"What's with the always?" The slurping sounds intensified. "Oh, that's their thing.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:44.280
They'll always love each other and whatever.

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:47.360
I would conservatively estimate that they have texted each other the word

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:50.740
"Always" four million times in the last year."

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:53.780
A couple more cars drove up, taking Michael and Elisa away.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:56.070
It was just Augustus and me now,

00:23:56.110 --> 00:24:01.530
watching Isaac and Monica, who proceeded apace as if they were not leaning against an actual place of worship.

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:09.030
His hand reached for her boob over her shirt and pawed at it, his palms still while his fingers moved around.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:15.599
I wondered if that felt good. Didn't seem like it would but I decided to forgive Isaac on the grounds that he was going blind.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:19.079
The senses must feast while there is yet hunger and whatever.

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:24.810
"Imagine taking that last drive to the hospital," I said, "The last time you'll ever drive a car."

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:27.780
Without looking over at me, Augustus said,

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:33.260
"You're killing my vibe here, Hazel Grace. I'm trying to observe young love and its many splendored awkwardnessess."

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:40.140
"I think he's hurting her boob," I said. "Yeah, it's difficult to ascertain whether he's trying to arouse her or perform a breast exam."

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:46.649
Then Augustus waters reached into a pocket and pulled out, of all things, a pack of cigarettes.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:50.100
He flipped it open and put a cigarette between his lips.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.560
"Are you serious?" I asked,

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:57.360
"You think that's cool? Oh my God, you just ruined the whole thing!"

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:00.120
"Which whole thing?" he asked, turning to me.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:04.144
The cigarette dangled unlit from the unsmiling corner of his mouth.

00:25:04.144 --> 00:25:07.160
"The whole thing where a boy who is not unattractive or

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.480
unintelligent or seemingly in any way

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:14.780
unacceptable stares at me and points out incorrect uses of literality and compares me to

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:20.200
actresses and asks me to watch a movie at his house, but of course there is always a hamartia.

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:24.240
And yours is that, oh my God, even though you had freaking cancer,

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:30.720
you give money to a company in exchange to a-- in exchange for the opportunity to acquire yet more cancer.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:36.089
Oh my God! Let me just assure you that not being able to breathe sucks!

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.879
Totally disappointing. Totally."

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:44.160
"A hamartia?" he asked, the cigarette still in his mouth. It tightened his jaw.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:50.610
He had a hell of a jawline, unfortunately. "A fatal flaw," I explained, turning away from him.

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:57.680
I stepped toward the curb, leaving Augustus Waters behind me, and then I heard a car start down the street. It was mom.

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:03.080
She'd been waiting for me to like, make friends or whatever. I felt this weird mix of

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.790
disappointment and anger welling up inside of me.

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:09.720
I don't even know what the feeling was really, just that there was a lot of it,

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:15.360
and I wanted to smack Augustus Waters and also replace my lungs with lungs that didn't suck at being lungs.

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:19.120
I was standing with my Chuck Taylors on the very edge of the curb,

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:25.680
the oxygen tank ball and chaining in the cart by my side and right as Mom pulled up, I felt a hand grab mine.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:29.120
I yanked my hand free, but turned back to him.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:35.700
"They don't kill you unless you light them," he said as Mom arrived at the curb, "And I've never lit one.

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:41.480
It's a metaphor. See, you put the killing thing right between your teeth, but you don't give it the power to do its killing."

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:47.639
"It's a metaphor," I said, dubious. Mom was just idling. "It's a metaphor," he said.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:53.440
"You choose your behaviors based on their metaphorical resonances?" I said.

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:57.870
"Oh, yeah," he smiled, the big, goofy real smile.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:00.449
"I'm a big believer in metaphor, Hazel Grace."

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:07.880
I turned to the car. Tapped on the window. It rolled down. "I'm going to a movie with Augustus Waters," I said.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:11.540
"Please record the next several episodes of the ANTM Marathon for me."

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:15.020
That's the end of chapter one!

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:21.240
The Fault in Our Stars is really, it's the story of Hazel and Augustus and their attempt to come to terms with that

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:27.900
soliloquy that Hazel has at the beginning of the book there, about how all of this will inevitably end in oblivion.

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:30.450
And their life together and

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:36.539
And their families and stuff. That's what the book is, so I hope you guys enjoyed the first chapter,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:40.400
and I hope that you like the the rest of the book as well if and when you have

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:42.500
the opportunity to read it. There are links in the doobly-doo

00:27:42.509 --> 00:27:48.300
if you want to preorder a signed copy. All copies will be signed. Before I go, I will show you the TFIOS-ometer.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:55.060
Let me tell you that I have made significant progress. I'm actually very nearly up to

00:27:55.220 --> 00:28:03.320
135,000, I think, although I haven't done a count recently, so just about 20,000 to go, maybe even a little less than 20,000... yeah.

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:04.660
I think just about like

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:10.380
15,000 to go, but I'm not positive. I don't... I don't know. I should do a count. It's somewhere between 15 and 20

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:11.820
um

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:13.900
but uh

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.880
Thank you all so much for making it possible

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:19.440
for me to sign all of these books and spend the last three months of my life watching

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:23.660
television and listening to audiobooks and signing my name over and over again. It's been fun.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:31.080
I'm really grateful to all of you, and I will read and respond to comments. This is good, this video is going to stay up.

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:35.900
It's going to be my vlogbrothers video for the day, for those of you who are late,

00:28:35.909 --> 00:28:39.200
I'm not getting punished because I cleared this with Hank, so I know that

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:42.600
it's longer than four minutes, and it's not edited or anything like that,

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:48.620
but it is going to free up time so that I can sign these remaining books. Which is very very very very

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:54.180
valuable to me right now. Oh look, it's Maureen Johnson with her dirty dirty text messages.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:58.470
hahahaha

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:04.639
That was actually a really good one of Maureen Johnson's better dirty jokes. Every time I do a live show,

00:29:04.889 --> 00:29:07.849
even ones that are going to live into perpetuity, Maureen Johnson

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:12.949
sends me a dirty dirty text message of some kind with some kind of joke that I can never repeat because they are so

00:29:13.889 --> 00:29:15.889
unfathomably filthy. um...

00:29:15.929 --> 00:29:19.819
So anyway, I hope you guys like the fault in Our Stars. Again,

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:24.289
there are pre-order links below if you want to preorder a signed copy. Certainly don't feel obligated...

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:28.489
Um, but you know if you want to I'm happy to sign a book for you.

00:29:28.489 --> 00:29:29.539
I'm going to sign them anyway.

00:29:29.539 --> 00:29:35.959
So, um, I'm happy if they go to a nice nerdfighter nerdfightastic  home instead of going to, like, Target.

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:40.309
Nothing against Target! Oh God, now Target's going to be mad at me. I love you Target!

00:29:40.309 --> 00:29:44.388
You're like my favorite of the two major Big-box stores. um...

00:29:45.179 --> 00:29:47.159
So anyway, thank you guys so much,

00:29:47.159 --> 00:29:51.618
and I'm gonna make a joke now that's going to make sense to very few of you,

00:29:51.619 --> 00:29:57.679
But I'm doing it as a favor to the person who got me a paper jogger. A nerd fighter gave me a paper jogger.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:01.100
Which is, Watch out for bears and

00:30:01.300 --> 00:30:05.900
and I'm also going to say to all of you an inside joke that you will get.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:10.880
French the llama for making my career possible, for the extraordinary

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:15.680
gift that you've given me by like watching this video into its 36th minute and

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:20.900
As always don't forget to be awesome and best wishes.

